Reason:
The urb is submitted without being anchored to `priv->urbs_anchor`. If `usb_kill_anchored_urbs()` is called concurrently (e.g., on device disconnect), the urb is not in the anchor list and will not be killed, causing a potential use-after-free or double-free when the completion handler runs or the urb is freed later.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
+	usb_anchor_urb(urb, &priv->urbs_anchor);
 	ret = usb_submit_urb(urb, GFP_ATOMIC);
+	if (!ret)
+		return;
+	usb_unanchor_urb(urb);
+
 	if (ret == -ENODEV)
 		netif_device_detach(netdev);
-	else if (ret)
+	else
 		netdev_err(...);
</text>
Reason:
Anchoring the urb before submission ensures it is tracked in the anchor list. If `usb_submit_urb` succeeds, the urb remains anchored and can be safely killed later; if it fails, the urb is immediately unanchored to avoid holding a stale reference. This prevents leaks and races with `usb_kill_anchored_urbs()`. The same pattern is applied to the interrupt callback to fix the same issue there.

